# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not yours to publish yet
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The manuscript is under submission and the DFIR Review note is under review.
# A repository is public the moment it is published, and a venue's preprint
# policy is not something to discover afterwards. Keep the paper out until it
# is accepted, then add the accepted version if the venue permits it.
#
# This is also why check_paper.py takes the manuscript as an argument rather
# than finding it in the tree: the verification scripts are the artefact, the
# paper is not.
*.tex
*.docx
*.pdf
paper.md
paper_for_test.md

# Correspondence. Reviews, rebuttals, cover letters and decision letters are
# between you and the venue.
review*
rebuttal*
cover*letter*
*decision*
correspondence/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated, and would become a second source of truth
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# claims.json and CLAIMS.md are recomputed from results/ by claims.py. A
# committed copy can drift from the records it was derived from, and then two
# files disagree and nothing says which is right. results/ is the source.
claims.json
CLAIMS.md

# Two files look generated and are not, so they are kept. Both record an
# observation that cannot be taken again, which is the opposite of a derived
# file: SHA256SUMS is the corpus as it was measured, and release_data.json is
# PyPI's release history as it stood on the date it was fetched. A release can
# be yanked and a package deleted, and then the provenance section rests on a
# snapshot nobody else can reproduce. claims.json is excluded because it is
# derived from results/, which is committed; these are derived from nothing
# that lives here.
!SHA256SUMS
!release_data.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environments
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A venv stores the absolute path of the interpreter that built it, so a
# committed one is useless to anyone else and large. setup_envs.py rebuilds
# them in about a minute.
envs/
envs-posix/
envs-win/
.venv/
venv/

__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Superseded
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# setup_envs.py works on both platforms. setup.ps1 walks a reader straight
# into the execution-policy wall, so shipping it invites them into a hole.
setup.ps1
setup.sh

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing in this study uses any, and no key has ever been written to disk
# here. The habit is still cheap and the cost of breaking it once is not.
.env
.env.*
*.key
*.pem
secrets.*
credentials*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Noise
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Word lock files carry the author's name.
~$*
*.tmp
*.bak
*.orig

# LaTeX build products
*.aux
*.log
*.out
*.synctex.gz
*.fls
*.fdb_latexmk
*.bbl
*.blg
est/

# Screenshots and scratch images
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg

# Files created by pasting a Windows settings path into bash, where > is a
# redirect. Harmless, and not something to publish.
/App
/Apps
/Advanced
/python3
/tzutil
/^C

# Editors and OS
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
